\documentclass{article}
\title{Night-Time Cooling in a Dense Urban Core}
\author{C. Beaumont and Y. Ibarra}
\date{July 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
The urban heat island effect keeps city centres warmer than their rural surroundings,
and the contrast is strongest a few hours after sunset.
We analyse a three year record of tower measurements from a dense downtown district
and quantify how quickly different land cover types release stored heat.

\section{Energy Balance}
The nocturnal cooling of an urban surface is governed by a simple balance.
Net radiative loss is offset by the release of heat stored during the day
and by anthropogenic emissions from traffic and air conditioning.
We write the surface energy budget as
\begin{align}
Q^* + Q_F &= Q_H + Q_E + \Delta Q_S \\
\Delta Q_S &= c_s \frac{\partial T_s}{\partial t}
\end{align}
where each term is averaged over the footprint of the tower.

\section{Measurements}
Three towers sample roof level, street canyon, and park sites.
Temperature and humidity are logged at one minute resolution
and flux measurements are averaged over thirty minutes.
We discard intervals with instrument faults or heavy precipitation.

\section{Data Quality}
Sensors are intercompared in a side by side deployment every six months.
Drift is corrected with a two point calibration against a reference sensor,
and the corrected records agree to within a tenth of a degree.
Roughly four percent of the record is lost to faults and power outages.

\section{Results}
Roof level sites cool fastest after sunset,
while street canyons remain warm well past midnight.
The park site tracks the rural reference within half a degree,
confirming the role of vegetation in night-time cooling.
Cooling rates are highest in the first two hours after sunset
and slow markedly once the canopy layer decouples from the roof level.

\section{Policy Implications}
The results support cooling strategies that increase vegetation cover
and reduce the thermal mass of paved surfaces in dense districts.
Targeted planting along east-west canyons gives the largest benefit
per tree in our simulations of the following summer.

\section{Conclusion}
Dense districts cool slowly because of storage and anthropogenic heat,
and land cover composition explains most of the site-to-site variance.
\end{document}

